That file is a derivative database. Its coordinates and codes come from
OurAirports (https://ourairports.com), which is public domain. Its IANA
timezone column comes from OpenFlights (https://openflights.org/data), which
is published under the Open Database License. Share-alike attaches to the
database rather than to the code that reads it, so the joined result is ODbL
even though half of its inputs are not.

The full ODbL text, the attributions, and what the licence does and does not
oblige are in src/rcta/data/LICENSE. In short: running this program does not
make your results ODbL -- a profile, an export, a plot or a paper is a
Produced Work and is yours to license as you please. The obligation applies
only if you redistribute airports.json itself.

The other bundled tables -- airlines.json, providers.json and
flight_providers.json -- are this project's own work and are MIT.

